Version starts at 1 (not 0) to distinguish from 'never saved' state.

### Background
The repository needs a method that only saves if the version matches, enabling optimistic locking at the data layer.

### Objective
Add saveWithVersion method to IAccountRepository interface.

### Files to Modify
- `src/repositories/interfaces.ts`

### Implementation Details
```typescript
export interface IAccountRepository {
  findById(id: string): Promise<Account | undefined>;
  save(account: Account): Promise<Account>;
  exists(id: string): Promise<boolean>;
  clear(): Promise<void>;

  /**
   * Save account only if current version matches expectedVersion.
   * Returns the saved account on success, null on version mismatch.
   * This enables optimistic locking to prevent lost updates.
   */
  saveWithVersion(account: Account, expectedVersion: number): Promise<Account | null>;
}
```

The method returns null (not throws) on mismatch to let the caller decide how to handle it.

### Background
The in-memory repository needs to implement the saveWithVersion method that checks version before saving.

### Objective
Implement saveWithVersion in InMemoryAccountRepository.

### Files to Modify
- `src/repositories/accountRepository.ts`

### Implementation Details
```typescript
/**
 * Save account only if version matches expected version.
 * Returns null if version mismatch (optimistic lock failure).
 */
async saveWithVersion(account: Account, expectedVersion: number): Promise<Account | null> {
  const existing = this.store.get(account.id);

  // For new accounts (not in store), expectedVersion should be 0
  if (!existing) {
    if (expectedVersion !== 0) {
      return null;  // Caller expected existing account
    }
    // New account, save it
    this.store.set(account.id, { ...account });
    return { ...account };
  }

  // For existing accounts, check version matches
  if (existing.version !== expectedVersion) {
    return null;  // Version mismatch, concurrent modification detected
  }

  // Version matches, safe to save
  this.store.set(account.id, { ...account });
  return { ...account };
}
```

Note: In-memory Maps are synchronous, so this is safe. A real database would use WHERE version = expectedVersion in the UPDATE.

### Background
The AccountService.applyEntry method needs to use saveWithVersion instead of save, incrementing the version and throwing ConflictError on mismatch.

### Objective
Update applyEntry to use optimistic locking with version checking.

### Files to Modify
- `src/services/accountService.ts`

### Implementation Details
```typescript
/**
 * Apply an entry to an account with optimistic locking.
 * @throws NotFoundError if account doesn't exist
 * @throws ConflictError if account was modified concurrently
 */
async applyEntry(entry: Entry): Promise<Account> {
  const account = await this.getAccount(entry.account_id);
  const expectedVersion = account.version;  // Capture before modification

  // Calculate new balance
  account.balance = calculateNewBalance(
    account.balance,
    account.direction,
    entry.direction,
    entry.amount
  );

  // Increment version and update timestamp
  account.version += 1;
  account.updated_at = new Date();

  // Attempt to save with version check
  const saved = await this.accountRepo.saveWithVersion(account, expectedVersion);

  if (!saved) {
    throw new ConflictError(
      `Account ${entry.account_id} was modified concurrently. Please retry.`
    );
  }

  return saved;
}
```

Make sure ConflictError is imported from '../errors/index.js'.

### Background
When optimistic locking fails, the transaction should automatically retry with fresh data rather than immediately failing. This provides a better user experience for legitimate concurrent access.

### Objective
Add retry logic to TransactionService.createTransaction for optimistic lock failures.

### Files to Modify
- `src/services/transactionService.ts`

### Implementation Details
```typescript
import { ConflictError } from '../errors/index.js';

const MAX_RETRIES = 3;

export class TransactionService {
  // ... constructor ...

  /**
   * Create a new transaction with automatic retry on optimistic lock failures.
   */
  async createTransaction(params: CreateTransactionParams): Promise<Transaction> {
    let lastError: Error | null = null;

    for (let attempt = 1; attempt <= MAX_RETRIES; attempt++) {
      try {
        return await this.attemptCreateTransaction(params);
      } catch (error) {
        if (error instanceof ConflictError && attempt < MAX_RETRIES) {
          // Optimistic lock failure, retry with fresh data
          lastError = error;
          continue;
        }
        // Not a lock failure, or max retries exceeded
        throw error;
      }
    }

    // Should not reach here, but satisfy TypeScript
    throw lastError ?? new Error('Transaction failed after retries');
  }

  /**
   * Single attempt to create a transaction (existing logic moved here).
   */
  private async attemptCreateTransaction(params: CreateTransactionParams): Promise<Transaction> {
    // ... move ALL existing createTransaction logic here ...
  }
}
```

Move the entire existing createTransaction body into attemptCreateTransaction, then have createTransaction wrap it with retry logic.

